tebelskis@msn.com (Joe Tebelskis) writes:

> I have some questions about numeric ranges in NetHack:
> 
> (1) Why do armor classes range from 10 (worst) to -40 (best), rather
> than from 0 (worst) to 50 (best)?  The latter would be much more
> straightforward.

The earlier versions of (A)D&D counted this way, and the 
combat system was quite popular in computer RPGs since 
it was simple and straightforward.  Note, however, that 
AC ranges from 128 to -127 (give or take 1 point, I can 
never remember whether it's this or 127 to -128).  

> (2) What exactly does the notation 18/** mean (for strength)?  How
> does this value compare to strength=25?  And does "**" mean "100" or
> "at least 100"?

Again, this is an AD&D relic, where for some strange reason 
fighters, and only fighters, could get more than a simple 18 
in strength.  18/** means 18/100 (you got a second % roll 
for this if your first roll was an 18).  STR scores like 
19,20, .. were related to the strength of giants; STR 19 
is better than STR 18/**, STR 25 is best, but the difference 
is not too big, IIRC.  

